            desc: 'The International Monetary Fund (IMF) approved the release of about $2.3 billion for Egypt on February 26, 2026, after completing the fifth and sixth reviews of its 46-month loan program.',
            content: "<p>The International Monetary Fund (IMF) announced on February 26, 2026, the completion of the fifth and sixth reviews of Egypt's 46-month loan program, paving the way for the release of about $2.3 billion. This disbursement consists of about $2 billion under the main loan program framework plus an additional $273 million from a related funding facility.</p><p>The IMF report noted that Egypt's broad-based economic recovery had lifted real Gross Domestic Product (GDP) growth to 4.4% in fiscal year 2024/2025, while inflation declined markedly to 11.9% in January 2026, a significant improvement compared to previous periods that had recorded inflation above 66%.</p><p>Nevertheless, Egypt's structural challenges remain substantial, with the country's external debt recorded at $169 billion, while external debt service obligations in 2026 alone are estimated at $27 billion — a fiscal burden absorbing more than half of total state revenue for debt and interest payments.</p><p>This IMF disbursement serves as a positive signal to international investors regarding Egypt's commitment to structural economic reform, including the flexible exchange rate policy previously implemented. However, economists continue to caution that the sustainability of long-term reforms will greatly determine Egypt's future economic stability, particularly amid ongoing regional geopolitical pressures.</p>",
