            content: "<p>Israel reclosed the Rafah border crossing between the Gaza Strip and Egypt on March 1, 2026, amid escalating joint Israeli-US attacks on Iran. Israeli security authorities stated the closure was part of 'necessary security measures' given the heating regional situation due to the conflict with Iran.</p><p>The Rafah crossing had previously reopened on a limited basis in early February 2026 as part of a Gaza ceasefire agreement facilitated by international mediation, allowing a small number of Palestinians including medical evacuees to cross the border into Egypt following years of severely limited access since the major closure in May 2024.</p><p>This border reclosure has raised fresh concerns among international humanitarian organizations regarding aid access and medical evacuation for Gaza residents still requiring urgent care. Egypt, which has long served as a mediator and key humanitarian channel for Gaza, again faces diplomatic challenges in maintaining stability in the North Sinai border region amid ongoing regional geopolitical uncertainty.</p><p>This situation adds to the complexity of challenges facing Egypt in balancing national security interests, diplomatic relations with Israel and Western countries, and humanitarian commitments toward the Palestinian people that continue to be a central concern of Egypt's foreign policy in the Middle East region.</p>",
